&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;v2 monorepo (archived): &lt;a href="https://github.com/marusyaganza/language-power-v2" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;language-power-v2&lt;/a&gt;, with the &lt;a href="https://lp-design-system.netlify.app/?path=/docs/design-system-colors--docs" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Storybook&lt;/a&gt; still deployed&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Professional contact: &lt;a href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/mariia-ganza/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;LinkedIn — Mariia Ganza&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Checked against official sites on November 2, 2026.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You need a pricing section, a hero and a footer. The useful question is not which library has the most components. It is which registry gives you a complete section that fits your project, and what happens when your agent installs it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;My shortlist: &lt;strong&gt;shadcn/ui for free application layouts, Shadcnblocks for a broad marketing catalog, Tailark for marketing pages, VibeUI for a visual pick → AI instruction → install workflow, and Magic UI for animated details.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Disclosure:&lt;/strong&gt; I build &lt;a href="https://vibeui.club" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;VibeUI&lt;/a&gt;. It is included below with its account requirement, free limit and paid restrictions. This is a documentation-based comparison, not a benchmark of installation speed or agent accuracy.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What a registry actually gives you
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A shadcn registry distributes source files and their declared dependencies into your project. It can contain a small control, a section or several files for a page. The format does not guarantee a shared visual style, license or dependency list.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The &lt;a href="https://ui.shadcn.com/docs/registry" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;official registry documentation&lt;/a&gt; describes the distribution model. Check the actual block and access terms before choosing a library for a client project.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Quick comparison
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Registry&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Where I would start&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Free access&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Paid boundary&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Install route&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;shadcn/ui&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Sidebars, login screens, dashboard layouts&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Official blocks are free and open source&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;No paid tier for the official blocks page&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;shadcn CLI&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Shadcnblocks&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Heroes, pricing, product and marketing sections&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Free blocks; Basic blocks require login&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Pro blocks and larger bundles&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Namespaced shadcn CLI; key for Pro&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Tailark&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Marketing websites with a selected design style&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Free pages, blocks and snippets&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Premium blocks, illustrations and page collections&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Registry workflow; follow the selected block's instructions&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;VibeUI&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Pick a designed block and hand it to Cursor or Claude Code&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Free account: 20 different components per month&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;PRO-only items and scenarios; unlimited copies with PRO&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Copy for AI or authenticated shadcn namespace&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Magic UI&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Animated text, backgrounds and UI effects&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Public component documentation and source&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Separate Pro blocks and templates&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;shadcn CLI&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The rows describe different products. A background effect and a whole dashboard are both installable source, but they do not replace the same amount of work.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  1. shadcn/ui: start here for application layouts
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The &lt;a href="https://ui.shadcn.com/blocks" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;official blocks page&lt;/a&gt; includes dashboards, collapsible sidebars and authentication layouts. It shows the files, preview and install command together.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For example, the documented dashboard command is:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;npx shadcn@latest add dashboard-01
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Choose it when:&lt;/strong&gt; you want an application layout built around the shadcn system and are comfortable doing the product-specific design yourself.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Check first:&lt;/strong&gt; the file list. A dashboard can add a page, several components and example data. An agent still needs instructions about where that page belongs and which sample data to replace.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  2. Shadcnblocks: a wide marketing catalog
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.shadcnblocks.com/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Shadcnblocks&lt;/a&gt; separates blocks, components and templates. Its categories include heroes, pricing, testimonials, product sections and dashboards. Free blocks are available, while Pro access and higher bundles cover more of the catalog.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The &lt;a href="https://www.shadcnblocks.com/shadcn-cli" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;CLI guide&lt;/a&gt; documents the registry configuration and API-key authentication for Pro. Its example command is:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;npx shadcn@latest add @shadcnblocks/hero1
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Configure the registry as the guide describes before using the command.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Choose it when:&lt;/strong&gt; you want many section variants to compare for a commercial site.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Check first:&lt;/strong&gt; whether the exact block is free or Pro, and which primitive-library build matches your project. A large catalog is useful only if the chosen section installs into your actual setup.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  3. Tailark: a marketing-site starting point
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://tailark.com/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Tailark&lt;/a&gt; focuses on marketing sites, with blocks, pages and illustrations in multiple styles. It states compatibility with Radix UI and Base UI.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Its &lt;a href="https://tailark.com/pricing" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;pricing page&lt;/a&gt; distinguishes free pages, blocks and snippets from premium blocks, illustrations and fuller page collections. Do not assume every page shown in a preview belongs to the free tier.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Choose it when:&lt;/strong&gt; the design of a marketing page is the main decision, and you want to work within one of its styles.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Check first:&lt;/strong&gt; access to the exact section and the installation instructions attached to it. I would select the hero and the following content section together before committing to a style.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  4. VibeUI: visual selection with an agent instruction
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://vibeui.club/blocks" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;VibeUI (vibeui.club)&lt;/a&gt; is a shadcn-compatible catalog of React/Tailwind components, landing blocks and animations. Each item has a &lt;strong&gt;Copy for AI&lt;/strong&gt; instruction with the install command, dependencies, what to preserve and what can change.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The useful distinction is the handoff: choose a preview, give the instruction to the agent, and ask it to install that component. The instruction is intended to preserve the selected design; it is not a guarantee that an agent will follow every rule.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The &lt;a href="https://vibeui.club/install" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;installation guide&lt;/a&gt; covers Copy for AI and an authenticated namespace. Use the command generated for your account instead of copying somebody else's temporary link.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Choose it when:&lt;/strong&gt; you already know which block you want and need to communicate that choice to an AI coding agent.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Check first:&lt;/strong&gt; a free account allows 20 different components per month. Some blocks, animations and page scenarios need PRO. The &lt;a href="https://vibeui.club/pricing" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;English pricing page&lt;/a&gt; lists $9 for a month or $69 for a year, paid once with crypto and no automatic renewal. Those restrictions matter if you prefer open-source-only tools or another payment method.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  5. Magic UI: add motion where it earns its place
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://magicui.design/docs/installation" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Magic UI's installation guide&lt;/a&gt; uses the shadcn workflow. Its catalog includes animated text, backgrounds, marquees and other effects; Pro blocks and templates are offered separately.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The guide's example is:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;npx shadcn@latest add @magicui/globe
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Choose it when:&lt;/strong&gt; the page layout is already settled and you need a specific animated element.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Check first:&lt;/strong&gt; the selected component's dependencies and motion behaviour. A visual effect is an addition to the page, not a substitute for its structure or content.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  How to hand a registry block to an agent
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A bare URL leaves too much room for interpretation. I would send a brief like this:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;Install the exact block using its documented command.
Read the registry files and dependency list before editing.
Put it on the existing landing page.
Keep its layout, spacing, typography and motion initially.
Replace only the copy, links and brand assets.
If installation or access fails, report the error before substituting another block.
Check the result on mobile and with reduced motion enabled.
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;For private registries, the &lt;a href="https://ui.shadcn.com/docs/registry/namespace" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;shadcn namespace documentation&lt;/a&gt; supports authentication headers using environment-variable placeholders. Keep the actual token out of the prompt, repository and screenshots.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  My selection rule
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Start with &lt;strong&gt;shadcn/ui&lt;/strong&gt; for the app shell. Compare &lt;strong&gt;Shadcnblocks&lt;/strong&gt; and &lt;strong&gt;Tailark&lt;/strong&gt; for marketing sections. Consider &lt;strong&gt;VibeUI&lt;/strong&gt; when the design-to-agent handoff is the problem. Add &lt;strong&gt;Magic UI&lt;/strong&gt; when you can name the effect the page needs.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Before paying or installing, check three things on the exact item: &lt;strong&gt;access, dependencies and license&lt;/strong&gt;. Then install one representative block and inspect it in your project. That tells you more than the library's total component count.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you build a data grid with TanStack Table in a shadcn app, most of the work is the grid UI, not the table logic. ReUI's data grid is that UI already written, on top of the same TanStack Table. So the useful question isn't which engine to use. It's how much grid UI you want to write, and how much of it you want to own.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I work on ReUI, so keep that in mind. I'll point out where plain TanStack Table is the better call.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What I checked
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I compared &lt;code&gt;@tanstack/react-table&lt;/code&gt; 9.3.1 with the ReUI data grid as the shadcn CLI served it on 3 Nov 2026. To see exactly what lands in a project, I ran &lt;code&gt;npx shadcn@latest view @reui/data-grid&lt;/code&gt; and counted the files.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;TanStack Table&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;ReUI data grid&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;What you install&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;one npm package&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;12 files in &lt;code&gt;components/reui/data-grid&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;UI included&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;none&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;table, header menus, filters, pagination, virtual table, cell selection&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Extra packages&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;none&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;TanStack Virtual, dnd-kit, Base UI or Radix&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Who maintains the UI&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;you&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;you, once it's installed&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;License&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;MIT&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;MIT, free&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  TanStack Table gives you state, not a table
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;TanStack Table describes itself as headless, with "full control over markup, styles, and behavior". You get sorting, filtering, grouping, selection and pagination as state and row models. It renders nothing.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For a sortable, paged table that's ideal. You map rows into the shadcn &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;Table&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt; you already have and you're done in an afternoon.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It gets heavier when the table has to behave like a grid: a menu on each column header, resize handles, pinned columns, keyboard focus moving between cells, virtualization (a separate package, TanStack Virtual), and copy and paste across a range of cells. TanStack Table leaves all of that UI to you on purpose.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  ReUI's data grid is that UI, already written
&lt;/h2&gt;



&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;npx shadcn@latest add @reui/data-grid
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;That adds 12 files and a little over 11,000 lines of TSX to your project. The four biggest files:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;File&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Lines (about)&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;What it covers&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;data-grid-table.tsx&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;3,100&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;the table, sticky headers, pinning, row states&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;data-grid-cell-selection.tsx&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;2,600&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;range selection, copy, paste, fill handle&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;data-grid.tsx&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;1,300&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;the provider and shared state&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;data-grid-table-virtual.tsx&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;1,200&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;row and column virtualization&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That number cuts both ways. It's a lot of grid work you skip. It's also a lot of code that now lives in your repo: you can change any line, and you also maintain every line.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;One caveat on the cell selection file: a range selection you write yourself could be far smaller if you skip the edge cases (pinned columns, virtualized rows, translated labels). The count shows what a complete version covers, not what you'd have to write.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You still write the TanStack Table part. The grid renders it:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;table&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useTable&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="na"&gt;features&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;dataGridFeatures&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nx"&gt;data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nx"&gt;columns&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;})&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;return &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;DataGrid&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;table&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;table&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;recordCount&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;length&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;DataGridContainer&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;DataGridTable&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;DataGridContainer&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;DataGridPagination&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;DataGrid&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Your columns, row models and state work the way they do in TanStack Table. Grid behaviour is turned on with &lt;code&gt;tableLayout&lt;/code&gt; props instead of built by hand: &lt;code&gt;headerSticky&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;columnsResizable&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;columnsPinnable&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;columnsDraggable&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;rowsPinnable&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;cellSelection&lt;/code&gt;. On top of that you get infinite scroll, tree and expandable rows, footer rows, inline editing, and one &lt;code&gt;i18n&lt;/code&gt; prop for every built-in string.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Four of the free examples:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://reui.io/components/data-grid/c-data-grid-31?utm_source=devto&amp;amp;utm_medium=social&amp;amp;utm_campaign=data-grid-comparison" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Ff108qsrglq0vbh8x0xnm.webp" alt="Spreadsheet editing" width="800" height="533"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://reui.io/components/data-grid/c-data-grid-32?utm_source=devto&amp;amp;utm_medium=social&amp;amp;utm_campaign=data-grid-comparison" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2F7mfho0xlhz15h23ljcyj.webp" alt="Column virtualization" width="800" height="533"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://reui.io/components/data-grid/c-data-grid-18?utm_source=devto&amp;amp;utm_medium=social&amp;amp;utm_campaign=data-grid-comparison" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2F33gh8xkofbqn96zy3zzi.webp" alt="Column controls" width="800" height="533"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://reui.io/components/data-grid/c-data-grid-30?utm_source=devto&amp;amp;utm_medium=social&amp;amp;utm_campaign=data-grid-comparison" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fqo2xzmym7uuhydcyjpgx.webp" alt="Tree rows" width="800" height="533"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;There are two versions, one on Base UI and one on Radix UI, so use the docs page that matches your project. What it doesn't have: pivot tables and Excel export.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Which one I'd pick
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;You need&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Pick&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;A sortable, paged table&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;TanStack Table with the shadcn &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;Table&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Every pixel your own, and time to build it&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;TanStack Table&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Pinning, resizing and virtualization in your own design system&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;ReUI data grid&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Spreadsheet-style range selection and editing&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;ReUI data grid&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Docs and every example: &lt;a href="https://reui.io/docs/components/base/data-grid?utm_source=devto&amp;amp;utm_medium=social&amp;amp;utm_campaign=data-grid-comparison" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://reui.io/docs/components/base/data-grid?utm_source=devto&amp;amp;utm_medium=social&amp;amp;utm_campaign=data-grid-comparison&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you've built a grid on TanStack Table, what took the longest: the column menus, virtualization, range selection, or something I didn't list?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Style React Server Components with typed props and no 'use client', style registry or build plugin, with the CSS inside the HTML React streams. This article shows how that works in a Next.js App Router app, measures what it costs against a plain CSS module, and lists what still has to run on the client.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;The examples here are screenshots; they run live in &lt;a href="https://www.box-kite.dev/blog/styling-server-components/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;the original article&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  The problem
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You moved a page to the App Router, and the first component that styles itself wants &lt;code&gt;'use client'&lt;/code&gt;. Runtime CSS-in-JS works by collecting styles while the tree renders and then writing them somewhere, either from an effect or through a context that a provider sets up. A Server Component has neither. It runs once, on the server, and React never calls an effect there.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The &lt;a href="https://nextjs.org/docs/app/guides/css-in-js" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Next.js guide to CSS-in-JS&lt;/a&gt; lists the popular libraries as supported &lt;strong&gt;in Client Components&lt;/strong&gt;. The recipe takes three pieces: a style registry, a &lt;code&gt;useServerInsertedHTML&lt;/code&gt; callback that flushes what the registry collected, and a &lt;code&gt;'use client'&lt;/code&gt; wrapper around the app. Where Server Component support has been added since, the theme provider is the part that had to go, because context does not exist on the server.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The usual ways around it each cost something real:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Put &lt;code&gt;'use client'&lt;/code&gt; on everything that styles itself.&lt;/strong&gt; The build passes. But every component with a style now ships its code to the browser and hydrates, which is the cost Server Components exist to remove.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Move to a compiler that extracts CSS at build time.&lt;/strong&gt; It ships no runtime, but it adds a bundler plugin, and a value only known at render time has to become a CSS variable written by hand.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Go back to stylesheets.&lt;/strong&gt; This is reliable, but the styles live in one file and the markup in another again, and nothing checks that a class name you wrote still exists.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Styling as rendering
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Box Kite takes a different route. Every CSS property is a typed prop, and in a Server Component it does not inject anything. Each rule a Box needs comes back as a React element, &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;style href precedence&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt;, rendered next to the markup. React 19 hoists those elements into &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;head&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt;, keeps one copy per &lt;code&gt;href&lt;/code&gt;, and orders them by &lt;code&gt;precedence&lt;/code&gt;. Styling becomes part of rendering, so it needs no effect, no provider and no registry.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Here is a Server Component page. There is no &lt;code&gt;'use client'&lt;/code&gt; in it and nothing above it:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;Flex&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;@box-kite/react/components/flex&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;Grid&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;@box-kite/react/components/grid&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;H1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;H2&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;P&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;Span&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;@box-kite/react/components/semantics&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;orders&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;4100&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;customer&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;Ana&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;status&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;Shipped&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;4101&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;customer&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;Ion&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;status&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;Packing&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;4102&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;customer&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;Mara&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;status&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;Delivered&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;];&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;default&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;OrdersPage&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;return &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
